1
00:00:00,550 --> 00:00:01,072
Welcome.

2
00:00:01,072 --> 00:00:07,342
This short tutorial shows how to use the education part of the What-If Tool: read a course plan,

3
00:00:07,342 --> 00:00:11,325
compare alternatives, test your own idea, and ask the AI why.

4
00:00:12,625 --> 00:00:20,667
For every week, the tool combines lectures, labs, homework, assignments and exams into one score from 0 to 90.

5
00:00:20,667 --> 00:00:24,688
It is the course's share of stress, not anyone's total.

6
00:00:24,688 --> 00:00:27,100
Warning is at 45, critical at 55.

7
00:00:28,400 --> 00:00:29,870
Open your course's page.

8
00:00:29,870 --> 00:00:35,875
The course card lists the semester, the current week, the students, and every assignment and exam.

9
00:00:37,175 --> 00:00:44,810
The Overview shows the plan as it stands, one point per week, with the warning and critical lines dashed.

10
00:00:44,810 --> 00:00:49,100
This plan peaks at 59.95 in week 11, right before the exam.

11
00:00:50,400 --> 00:00:53,580
Below, every scenario is ranked by its peak.

12
00:00:53,580 --> 00:00:59,217
Four were generated automatically, and the best brings the peak down to 49.11.

13
00:00:59,217 --> 00:01:02,325
Scenarios people build are ranked here too.

14
00:01:03,625 --> 00:01:06,464
Click a scenario's tab to see it in detail.

15
00:01:06,464 --> 00:01:10,822
The solid line is the scenario, the dashed line the original plan.

16
00:01:10,822 --> 00:01:13,925
When one suits you, press Select this scenario.

17
00:01:15,225 --> 00:01:20,381
In the weekly breakdown, click a week to open the seven components behind its score,

18
00:01:20,381 --> 00:01:24,800
from homework and the exam to fatigue carried over from the week before.

19
00:01:26,100 --> 00:01:28,662
To test your own idea, open New what-if.

20
00:01:28,662 --> 00:01:33,723
Pick an adjustment, fill in the details and a reason, and press Add adjustment.

21
00:01:33,723 --> 00:01:39,231
Here we cut 6 hours of homework from week 11 and give the project report another week.

22
00:01:39,231 --> 00:01:41,025
Name it, and press Simulate.

23
00:01:42,325 --> 00:01:45,486
The result opens as its own tab.

24
00:01:45,486 --> 00:01:48,944
The peak drops from 59.95 to 56.05.

25
00:01:48,944 --> 00:01:50,525
This one helped.

26
00:01:51,825 --> 00:01:53,060
But always check.

27
00:01:53,060 --> 00:01:58,945
Moving the exam a week later applied all four changes, yet the peak rose by 0.14.

28
00:01:58,945 --> 00:02:06,500
The tool says so plainly, and every change shows whether it was applied, partially applied, or rejected.

29
00:02:07,800 --> 00:02:11,054
When a result surprises you, open the chat and ask.

30
00:02:11,054 --> 00:02:14,499
The assistant knows the course and the tab you are on.

31
00:02:14,499 --> 00:02:20,752
Here it explains that the exam now stacks on top of week 12's workload, and suggests alternatives.

32
00:02:20,752 --> 00:02:21,773
It runs locally.

33
00:02:21,773 --> 00:02:24,325
Treat it as advice, and check its facts.

34
00:02:25,625 --> 00:02:32,525
So: read the Overview, compare the scenarios, build your own what-if, and check the result, asking the AI why.

35
00:02:33,825 --> 00:02:37,343
The written user guide covers every step in more detail.

36
00:02:37,343 --> 00:02:38,600
Thanks for watching.
